{
  "concept": "Distance to an object can be recovered from angles alone — either by combining one known height with a horizon reference (single observer), or by combining two viewpoints separated by a known baseline (triangulation) — the geometric root of stereo depth.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Outer rectangular panel frame (black stroke)",
    "White sky region in upper part of panel",
    "Cyan/turquoise sea region filling lower part of panel",
    "Ochre/brown coast and cliff mass on the left, with an irregular hand-drawn coastline edge separating land from sea",
    "Horizon line: sharp horizontal boundary between white sky and cyan sea, extending across the panel",
    "Single-observer mode: white circle (observer's eye) outlined in black, sitting at the cliff top above the horizon",
    "Horizontal dashed/solid reference ray from the observer's eye toward the horizon (line of sight to horizon)",
    "Line of sight from observer's eye down to the boat",
    "Depression angle α marked at the observer between horizontal reference ray and line of sight to boat",
    "Alternate angle α marked at the boat between the line of sight and the horizontal sea-surface line",
    "Vertical segment labeled h from the observer's eye down to the water/base level, with small grey right-angle square at its foot",
    "Horizontal baseline segment from foot of h out to the boat, labeled 'd?'",
    "Two-observer mode: two white circles (observer A upper-left on the cliff, observer B lower-right on the slope)",
    "Straight segment between the two observers labeled t (known separation/baseline), with grey right-angle marker on it",
    "Sight line from observer A to the boat with angle α marked at A between the baseline and the sight line",
    "Sight line from observer B to the boat with angle β marked at B",
    "Label 'd?' on the sight line from A to the boat (the unknown range)",
    "Hand-drawn sailboat on the right (hull, mast, triangular sail) sitting on the sea",
    "Text labels: h, t, d?, α, β",
    "Readout box showing measured angles and the computed distance d",
    "Uncertainty wedge / shaded band around the boat showing the range of possible distances for a given angular measurement error"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "methodToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Both methods solve the same unknown range d, but one substitutes a known vertical height for a horizontal baseline — the two are geometrically the same triangle-solving trick.",
      "label": "Measurement method",
      "options": ["Single observer (height + horizon)", "Two observers (baseline)"],
      "default": "Single observer (height + horizon)"
    },
    {
      "id": "observerHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A taller vantage point produces a larger depression angle for the same boat, so height acts as the 'baseline' that makes the angle measurable at all.",
      "label": "Observer height h (m)",
      "range": [2, 120, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "baseline",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A wider separation between the two observers opens the triangle at the boat, making the intersection of the two sight lines better conditioned — exactly the role of the interocular distance in stereo.",
      "label": "Observer separation t (m)",
      "range": [5, 250, 5],
      "default": 60
    },
    {
      "id": "boatDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "As the boat sails farther out, both the depression angle and the angular difference between the two observers shrink toward zero — distant depth is encoded in vanishingly small angles.",
      "label": "True boat distance d (m)",
      "range": [50, 3000, 10],
      "default": 600
    },
    {
      "id": "angleError",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "A fixed angular measurement error produces a distance error that grows roughly with the square of the distance and shrinks with height or baseline — the depth-uncertainty law behind stereo vision.",
      "label": "Angle measurement error (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The question: how far is the boat?",
      "narration": "An observer on a cliff sees a sailboat on the sea. The only unknown is d, the horizontal distance to the boat; everything else must be measured with angles.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 25, "boatDistance": 600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The horizon gives you a horizontal reference",
      "narration": "The line to the horizon is (very nearly) horizontal, so the angle α below it is the depression angle to the boat. The same α reappears at the boat as an alternate angle, closing a right triangle with legs h and d.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 25, "boatDistance": 600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Height sets the scale: d = h / tan α",
      "narration": "Raise the observer and the depression angle grows for the same boat. Height is what converts a measured angle into a metric distance — without a known h, α alone tells you nothing.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 90, "boatDistance": 600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Far boats squeeze the angle",
      "narration": "Push the boat out to sea: α collapses toward the horizon. Distant objects differ by only a fraction of a degree, which is why far depth is hard to measure.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Single observer (height + horizon)", "observerHeight": 25, "boatDistance": 2600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Swap the known height for a known baseline",
      "narration": "Now two observers a distance t apart each measure a bearing to the boat, α and β. The boat lies where the two sight lines cross — no horizon and no height needed.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (baseline)", "baseline": 60, "boatDistance": 600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A wider baseline sharpens the fix",
      "narration": "Increase the separation t and the two sight lines cross at a larger angle, so the intersection is far better localized. This is precisely why eyes — and stereo cameras — are set apart.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (baseline)", "baseline": 220, "boatDistance": 600, "angleError": 0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Angular error becomes depth error",
      "narration": "Add a small error to each measured angle and watch the uncertainty wedge at the boat. The same error is tolerable up close but explodes far away, and shrinks again when h or t is large.",
      "state": { "methodToggle": "Two observers (baseline)", "baseline": 60, "boatDistance": 2000, "angleError": 1 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Angles alone never give distance — you need one known length: a height above the water or a separation between two viewpoints. That known length is the baseline, and depth precision improves with baseline and degrades with the square of the distance.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single wide panel (replacing the original two) with a hand-drawn, thick-black-outline style. Upper region white sky, lower region bright turquoise sea, with a crisp horizontal horizon line about one third down. An ochre/brown cliff with a wobbly, hand-sketched silhouette occupies the left side and continues as a submerged slope into the sea. A hand-drawn sailboat (rounded hull, vertical mast, single triangular white sail) floats on the right, its horizontal position driven by the boat-distance slider. In single-observer mode: a white black-outlined circle sits atop the cliff above the horizon; a thin horizontal ray runs right from it to the horizon; a second straight line runs from it down to the boat's waterline, with the angle α arced between them near the eye and the alternate angle α arced at the boat; a vertical line labeled h drops from the eye to the base with a small grey right-angle square, and a horizontal line labeled 'd?' runs from that base point to the boat. In two-observer mode: two white circles on the cliff face, an upper-left one and a lower-right one, joined by a straight segment labeled t with a grey right-angle tick; each circle sends a straight sight line to the boat, angle α arced at the upper observer between baseline and sight line, β arced at the lower observer, and 'd?' labeled along the upper sight line. Labels in a serif math face (h, t, d?, α, β). A small light readout panel in a corner shows measured α (and β), the known h or t, and the computed d compared with the true d. When angle error is non-zero, draw a translucent red/orange wedge or band of possible boat positions along the sight direction, visibly widening as the boat moves out and narrowing as h or t increases."
}

